1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:05,201
On the afternoon Amy Mihaljevic disappeared,
her mother received a phone call from her.

2
00:00:05,901 --> 00:00:09,243
An FBI account describes
Amy saying she was okay.

3
00:00:10,063 --> 00:00:12,184
Her mother believed she was calling from home.

4
00:00:12,884 --> 00:00:17,896
According to FBI Special Agent Vicki
Anderson, Amy was already with the person

5
00:00:18,196 --> 00:00:18,936
who had taken her.

6
00:00:19,767 --> 00:00:24,369
A familiar kind of check-in had given
her mother a mistaken picture of where

7
00:00:24,409 --> 00:00:29,111
her daughter was. Amy was ten years
old and lived in Bay Village, Ohio.

8
00:00:30,032 --> 00:00:31,593
She enjoyed riding her bicycle.

9
00:00:32,454 --> 00:00:37,317
In a family interview years later, her
father, Mark, remembered another part

10
00:00:37,357 --> 00:00:42,401
of her life: the horses she rode, and the
way she played with their dog, Jake.

11
00:00:43,101 --> 00:00:46,463
She had taught him to jump over
her legs as though he were a horse.

12
00:00:47,554 --> 00:00:52,418
It is a small memory of Amy doing something
she loved, before her name became

13
00:00:52,478 --> 00:00:53,859
attached to an investigation.

14
00:00:54,859 --> 00:00:59,703
Before October 27, 1989,
someone had contacted Amy

15
00:00:59,843 --> 00:01:01,904
with a plan that involved her mother.

16
00:01:02,604 --> 00:01:07,007
The caller said Amy could help choose
a gift to celebrate a promotion at work.

17
00:01:07,707 --> 00:01:11,610
Bay Village Police place that contact
weeks before the disappearance.

18
00:01:12,310 --> 00:01:16,132
The meeting was arranged for after
school, at a nearby shopping center.

19
00:01:16,832 --> 00:01:22,216
The promise of a present for her mother was
the reason Amy had been given for going there.

20
00:01:23,226 --> 00:01:25,636
That Friday, Amy left school on foot.

21
00:01:26,336 --> 00:01:30,998
The FBI says she went to Bay Village
Square and was seen outside a business,

22
00:01:31,378 --> 00:01:32,759
apparently waiting for someone.

23
00:01:33,549 --> 00:01:37,931
The police account describes witnesses
seeing her speak with an unidentified

24
00:01:37,991 --> 00:01:40,481
man, then walk through the parking lot with him.

25
00:01:41,542 --> 00:01:43,943
No vehicle was seen, according to that account.

26
00:01:44,643 --> 00:01:48,494
The sighting establishes a public
point in the sequence, but it does not

27
00:01:48,554 --> 00:01:50,235
reveal where Amy went afterward.

28
00:01:51,075 --> 00:01:52,637
Her bicycle was still at school.

29
00:01:53,337 --> 00:01:58,681
In a June 2026 interview, her brother,
Jason, recalled noticing it there

30
00:01:58,881 --> 00:02:00,423
after his own classes ended.

31
00:02:01,133 --> 00:02:04,996
News 5's account of the day says
their mother later found the bicycle

32
00:02:05,056 --> 00:02:08,579
at the school and went to the
police to report Amy missing.

33
00:02:09,350 --> 00:02:14,082
By then, the expectation that she
was safely home had given way

34
00:02:14,343 --> 00:02:19,395
to a search for her. Bay Village Police
and the FBI began investigating.

35
00:02:20,115 --> 00:02:22,816
Public appeals spread
beyond the shopping center.

36
00:02:23,516 --> 00:02:27,857
News 5's reporting describes
missing-person posters in shop windows

37
00:02:28,337 --> 00:02:30,938
and flyers distributed around the region.

38
00:02:31,638 --> 00:02:36,300
A local Pizza Hut put Amy's poster
and a composite drawing on its boxes.

39
00:02:37,030 --> 00:02:41,352
Those efforts carried the appeal into
ordinary places where someone might

40
00:02:41,412 --> 00:02:44,613
recognize her or remember the man seen with her.

41
00:02:45,733 --> 00:02:49,305
Inside the investigation,
information also accumulated.

42
00:02:50,005 --> 00:02:54,368
The same reporting describes officers
and federal agents working through

43
00:02:54,408 --> 00:02:59,532
leads, with a law-enforcement computer
system strained by the volume of inquiries.

44
00:03:00,233 --> 00:03:04,946
People were being asked to look back at
one afternoon, while investigators had

45
00:03:04,986 --> 00:03:06,347
to organize what came in.

46
00:03:07,348 --> 00:03:10,470
The public search continued,
but Amy did not return home.

47
00:03:11,782 --> 00:03:16,806
On February 8, 1990, a jogger
found Amy's body in a field

48
00:03:16,986 --> 00:03:18,387
in rural Ashland County.

49
00:03:19,087 --> 00:03:21,949
She was wearing the clothing in
which she had last been seen.

50
00:03:22,649 --> 00:03:26,552
The search for a missing child had
become an investigation into her death.

51
00:03:27,252 --> 00:03:32,156
The discovery gave investigators
another location to examine, beyond

52
00:03:32,196 --> 00:03:34,598
the shopping center where she had disappeared.

53
00:03:35,608 --> 00:03:39,069
Some of the things believed to have
been with her were never recovered.

54
00:03:39,880 --> 00:03:45,222
The FBI lists a turquoise horse earring,
black ankle boots and a black leather binder.

55
00:03:45,922 --> 00:03:48,963
Those objects remain part of its public appeal.

56
00:03:49,663 --> 00:03:53,885
They are details someone might recognize,
but the published record does not

57
00:03:53,945 --> 00:03:55,325
establish what happened to them.

58
00:03:56,025 --> 00:04:01,477
Their absence cannot explain, by itself,
who took Amy or where she was killed.

59
00:04:02,457 --> 00:04:07,061
Years later, investigators asked the public
to look closely at another object.

60
00:04:08,061 --> 00:04:12,945
At a 2016 news conference, authorities
displayed a homemade-looking curtain

61
00:04:13,485 --> 00:04:16,387
found in the field where Amy's
body had been discovered.

62
00:04:17,258 --> 00:04:22,642
News 5 reported a connection between
hair on the curtain and the family's dog.

63
00:04:23,342 --> 00:04:27,515
The appeal was for recognition of the
textile: where it might have come from,

64
00:04:28,095 --> 00:04:29,016
or who might have made it.

65
00:04:29,956 --> 00:04:31,537
The release brought responses.

66
00:04:32,248 --> 00:04:37,352
Cleveland 19 reported that the
prosecutor's office received calls and tips

67
00:04:37,532 --> 00:04:40,914
afterward, and that law enforcement
was following them up.

68
00:04:41,895 --> 00:04:46,298
The curtain offered a possible
connection between a physical object

69
00:04:46,518 --> 00:04:50,380
and information someone outside
the investigation might hold.

70
00:04:51,441 --> 00:04:56,645
Its owner and origin were still questions;
the public release did not announce

71
00:04:56,685 --> 00:04:58,186
that either had been established.

72
00:04:59,186 --> 00:05:03,869
The investigation also continued through
interviews and renewed publicity.

73
00:05:04,660 --> 00:05:09,733
In a report marking thirty years since
Amy disappeared, FBI Special Agent

74
00:05:09,813 --> 00:05:14,616
Lisa Hack described more than
a hundred interviews during her six years

75
00:05:14,696 --> 00:05:15,577
working on the case.

76
00:05:16,587 --> 00:05:19,009
She said the files filled seventy volumes.

77
00:05:19,729 --> 00:05:24,632
The FBI had also put up billboards around
Cleveland and in neighboring states,

78
00:05:25,273 --> 00:05:26,834
asking again for information.

79
00:05:27,534 --> 00:05:30,596
Those were accounts of continuing
work, not a solved case.

80
00:05:31,857 --> 00:05:36,989
In 2021, Bay Village Sergeant Edward
Chapman described a further

81
00:05:37,049 --> 00:05:38,630
reason for examining the curtain.

82
00:05:39,371 --> 00:05:43,253
He told News 5 that testing
had identified Amy's hair on it.

83
00:05:44,234 --> 00:05:49,617
Investigators again asked whether anyone
recognized the unusual, apparently

84
00:05:49,677 --> 00:05:50,758
handmade textile.

85
00:05:51,768 --> 00:05:55,691
The new statement strengthened their
public explanation for its relevance.

86
00:05:56,451 --> 00:06:00,674
It did not supply a public account of
where the crime occurred or identify

87
00:06:00,714 --> 00:06:06,176
the person responsible. The next reported
testing developments involved Amy's clothing.

88
00:06:07,197 --> 00:06:11,738
In 2024, police said retesting
had detected male DNA,

89
00:06:12,148 --> 00:06:15,029
but the amount was too
small to create a profile.

90
00:06:15,889 --> 00:06:21,081
That distinction matters: detecting
material and producing a usable profile

91
00:06:21,481 --> 00:06:22,522
were different outcomes.

92
00:06:23,252 --> 00:06:27,713
Investigators were hoping that more
sensitive procedures might eventually

93
00:06:27,773 --> 00:06:29,494
allow further work on the sample.

94
00:06:30,244 --> 00:06:32,505
They had not announced
an identification from it.

95
00:06:33,405 --> 00:06:35,607
Another round of work involved separate hairs.

96
00:06:36,307 --> 00:06:41,330
News 5 reported that an FBI laboratory
had noticed hairs on evidence slides

97
00:06:41,790 --> 00:06:44,292
previously thought to contain clothing fibers.

98
00:06:44,992 --> 00:06:48,314
They were sent to a private
laboratory in California for testing.

99
00:06:49,014 --> 00:06:52,957
Chapman cautioned that the hairs did
not necessarily belong to the person

100
00:06:53,017 --> 00:06:57,390
responsible, and that contamination
was possible after years of handling.

101
00:06:58,210 --> 00:07:01,973
These were separate from the small
DNA sample on Amy's clothing.

102
00:07:03,013 --> 00:07:08,487
In January 2026, Cleveland 19
reported a setback in that hair testing.

103
00:07:09,426 --> 00:07:14,310
Detective Sergeant Jay Elish said
Astrea Forensics could not obtain

104
00:07:14,350 --> 00:07:16,531
a profile from the submitted hairs.

105
00:07:17,361 --> 00:07:20,683
The material was likely too
small or too degraded.

106
00:07:21,484 --> 00:07:26,267
Investigators were continuing with
DNA work, but this particular effort had

107
00:07:26,367 --> 00:07:28,048
not produced the result they wanted.

108
00:07:28,748 --> 00:07:34,132
That outcome concerned the hairs; it did not
erase the separate clothing-DNA finding.

109
00:07:35,132 --> 00:07:39,765
By June, Police Chief Robert Gillespie
was describing the resources needed

110
00:07:40,185 --> 00:07:41,486
to keep testing evidence.

111
00:07:42,506 --> 00:07:47,099
He told Cleveland 19 that work was
continuing, although he could not discuss

112
00:07:47,359 --> 00:07:48,660
every item being tested.

113
00:07:49,720 --> 00:07:54,843
He explained that the Amy Fund
supplemented other funding so investigators

114
00:07:54,903 --> 00:07:57,904
could respond when testing opportunities arose.

115
00:07:58,815 --> 00:08:03,457
Bay Village's own community announcement
also describes the fund as supporting

116
00:08:03,537 --> 00:08:06,399
investigative costs, including DNA testing.

117
00:08:07,390 --> 00:08:11,312
Financial support helps that work
continue; it is not a test result.

118
00:08:12,312 --> 00:08:17,654
As of our October 16, 2026 source
check, Bay Village's public case

119
00:08:17,714 --> 00:08:20,635
page describes the investigation as ongoing.

120
00:08:21,335 --> 00:08:24,676
Police still ask for help identifying
the curtain and blanket.

121
00:08:25,437 --> 00:08:29,538
The FBI continues to offer a reward
of up to twenty-five thousand dollars

122
00:08:29,618 --> 00:08:35,000
for information leading to the arrest and
conviction of those responsible for Amy's death.

123
00:08:35,700 --> 00:08:40,482
These remain public requests for
information, with the case unresolved

124
00:08:40,542 --> 00:08:41,582
in the sources reviewed.

125
00:08:42,582 --> 00:08:46,755
For Amy's family, those records
sit alongside memories of a child.

126
00:08:47,455 --> 00:08:51,398
In the thirty-year interview, her father
described still thinking about her

127
00:08:51,478 --> 00:08:52,459
several times a week.

128
00:08:53,300 --> 00:08:57,984
The bicycle, the time with Jake,
the things she enjoyed: those belong

129
00:08:58,004 --> 00:09:00,866
to Amy's life, beyond the evidence left behind.

130
00:09:01,627 --> 00:09:03,048
Amy is a daughter and a sister.

131
00:09:03,748 --> 00:09:08,191
The public appeal remains open,
and the effort to find answers continues.
